<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nefertari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1255 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Nefertari Meritmut, Naptera</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nefertari, also Nefertari Meritmut, was an Egyptian queen and the first of the Great Royal Wives of Ramesses the Great, whom she married before he became king. She bore at least four sons and two daughters, among them the crown prince Amun-her-khepeshef. Able to read and write hieroglyphs, a rare skill, she corresponded with other royals: tablets from the Hittite capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusas</a> preserve her letters, as Naptera, to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a> and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puduhepa">Puduhepa</a>. Ramesses built a temple for her at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a> beside his own. She was buried in QV66, one of the largest tombs of the Valley of the Queens, robbed in antiquity and excavated by Ernesto Schiaparelli in 1904.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nefertari.jpg" alt="Nefertari" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Doudoroff · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Nefertari_(52785688763).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The name Nefertari is often translated as beautiful companion, and Meritmut as beloved of the goddess Mut, but a study of similar personal names argues that Nefertari is better rendered as the most beautiful one. Her family background is unknown, and there is no conclusive evidence linking her to the royal family of the 18th Dynasty. She married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> before he became king. Her sons included Meryatum, High Priest of Re in Heliopolis, and Pareherwenemef, who served in the army. Meritamen and Henuttawy are thought to be her daughters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Nefertari appears beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> in official scenes from the first year of his reign. In the tomb of Nebwenenef she stands behind him as he appoints Nebwenenef High Priest of Amun at Abydos, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a> she leads the royal children. Under Ramesses the title Hereditary Princess was conferred on no queen who was not a king's daughter except Nefertari and the Queen Mother Tuya. Stamp seals bearing her name found at Qantir make her the only person besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> to whom such seals are attributed.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a> the small temple was dedicated to Nefertari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a> of Ibshek, and its dedication text says it was made for the Great Royal Wife for whose sake the sun shines. Two colossal standing statues of her in front of it equal those of Ramesses in size, and the temple was in use before the twentieth year of his reign. The small temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesseum">Ramesseum</a> was dedicated to her and to Tuya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nefertari died before the twenty-fourth year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>, since a stela of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heqanakht">Heqanakht</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-kush">Viceroy of Kush</a>, whose tenure ended that year, shows her already venerated as a goddess. Her tomb, QV66, covers 520 square metres and is covered with pictures of her. Ramesses is not shown in it, though she is the only queen depicted in his own tomb. The tomb was robbed in antiquity and was rediscovered and excavated by Ernesto Schiaparelli in 1904. Items the robbers overlooked are now in the Boston Museum of Fine Arts and the Egyptian Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. A pair of mummified legs found there, now in the Museo Egizio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, are most probably her knees.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nefertari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Great Temple was dedicated to Ramesses II himself, and the Small Temple to his chief wife, Queen <b>Nefertari</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Queens such as Tiye and <b>Nefertari</b> had real influence, the God’s Wife of Amun was the highest priestess of Amun at Thebes, and Hatshepsut ruled as king.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhmim">Akhmim</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Valley of the Queens, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.56,31.75&amp;year=-1265" aria-label="Akhmim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Nefertari"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5134 5293h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5134 5293h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.56,31.75&amp;year=-1265">What was here in 1265 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
